## Test Smell Audit — Academic 19-Smell Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Target:** `DataAccess.IntegrationTests/UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`  
**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · .NET 10  
**Integration calibration applied:** Class is marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test (Smell #3)

**Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // ← 3-second hard sleep
```

**Why it's still a smell in integration tests:** Even for integration scope, `Thread.Sleep` introduces non-determinism — pass on fast hardware, fail on slow CI runners. It also adds 3s of wall-clock time per invocation.

**Fix — use a polling wait with timeout:**
```csharp
var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
while (sw.Elapsed < timeout)
{
    if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
        break;
    await Task.Delay(50);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
    "Notification was not sent within timeout.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky builds, slow suite, false failures in CI.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic (Smell #1)

**Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** Only one branch executes per run. The `PremiumUser` path (or the `else` path) may **never** be tested, creating a hidden gap.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName() { /* assert Name */ }

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount() { /* insert premium, assert DiscountRate */ }
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Silently passing test that covers only half the behavior.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test / Unknown Test (Smell #4)

**Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Problem:** This test passes as long as no unhandled exception is thrown. Silent data corruption, constraint violations swallowed internally, or wrong row counts are all invisible.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var all = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, all.Count, "All bulk-inserted users should be retrievable.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — 100% pass rate with zero validated behavior.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette (Extended Smell)

**Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions, none with a failure message. When one fails in CI, the stack trace points to a line number but the reason isn't self-documenting.

**Fix — add messages to at least the non-obvious assertions:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Persisted user should have a positive database ID.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower diagnosis during CI failures — minor but compounds across a growing suite.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The following tests are **well-designed** and demonstrate good integration-test practices:

| Method | Why it's good |
|--------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange/act/assert, single behavior under test |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Asserts absence correctly with `Assert.IsNull` |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count is self-documenting (3 inserted → assert 3), uses `CollectionAssert` |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Real concurrency test with clear post-condition |

The in-memory SQLite setup is also commendable — it avoids the **Mystery Guest** smell by being hermetic and self-contained.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | Add count assertion to `BulkInsert` | ~2 min |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | Split `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` into 2 tests | ~5 min |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with polling/timeout loop | ~10 min |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | Add failure messages to multi-assert tests | ~5 min |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 8 tests are clean. The 3 high-severity smells are all straightforward to fix and will meaningfully improve reliability and diagnostic speed. This is a solid integration suite with a few well-known anti-patterns to clean up.
